1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,560
Last year we made it significantly easier to find DOMXSS with a new tool called DOMInvader.

2
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:28,740
This year we've improved DOMInvader to make finding client-side prototype pollution as easy as a couple of clicks.

3
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:33,140
So what exactly is client-side prototype pollution?

4
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:45,600
It is a vulnerability that occurs when you merge an object with a user-controlled JSON object or an object generated from the query or hash parameters.

5
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,820
And the merge operation doesn't sanitize the keys.

6
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:57,100
This enables the attacker to use property keys like underscore underscore proto underscore underscore,

7
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:03,820
which allows them to create arbitrary assignments to the object prototype or other global prototypes.

8
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:09,280
When this happens, it's referred to as a prototype pollution source.

9
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,940
The following sample code demonstrates this.

10
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:18,840
The snippet of code looks through all the parameters in the query string.

11
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:29,260
URL decodes the parameter name, creates an object and assigns a property name that an attacker can control as well as a property value.

12
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,740
And that's the basis of how prototype pollution sources occur.

13
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:42,480
In order to exploit prototype pollution, you need both a source and a gadget.

14
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:50,560
A prototype pollution gadget occurs when the property is used in a dangerous way without filtering.

15
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:54,740
For example, a site might do the following.

16
00:01:54,740 --> 00:02:00,640
At first glance, it might not look like there's a problem here.

17
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:12,360
The object doesn't contain any properties, but the JavaScript engine will look at the object prototype for the HTML property if it doesn't exist on the current object.

18
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:17,740
Then this leads to a prototype pollution gadget called HTML.

19
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:22,560
Let's see what happens when we modify the object prototype.

20
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:38,840
This results in the object prototype dot HTML property being used instead of the HTML property of the my object object literal.

21
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:45,900
A developer will assume such properties are not user controlled and thus leads to XSS.

22
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:52,500
DomInvader can help find both a prototype pollution source and gadget for you.

23
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:55,020
Let's see how we can find them.

24
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:05,460
By default, client-side prototype pollution scanning is turned off.

25
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:08,400
This is because it can cause site breakage.

26
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:14,080
However, if you want to find some prototype pollution sources, we need to switch it on.

27
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:20,280
So, head over to the DomInvader icon and switch on prototype pollution.

28
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:22,720
And hit reload.

29
