And you can see the test property has been injected into the object prototype.

67
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:28,100
And you can confirm that this site is indeed vulnerable to prototype pollution.

68
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:38,360
You can find gadgets in two different ways.

69
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:41,900
The first is to use the scan for gadgets button.

70
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:46,560
This button appears on the source tree when you've found a prototype pollution source.

71
00:06:46,560 --> 00:06:54,360
The advantage of using this button is that DomInvader combines the source and the gadget when producing an exploit.

72
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:59,960
So, let's click on the scan for gadgets button on the prototype pollution source we found earlier.

73
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:09,460
You'll notice when clicking on the button, DomInvader will open a new window and show a progress bar.

74
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:16,240
When the scan is complete, DomInvader will report the gadgets it's found and the icon will change.

75
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:21,060
Let's open up DevTools and the DomInvader tab.

76
00:07:21,060 --> 00:07:30,400
As you can see, DomInvader has found an inner HTML sink.

77
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:35,400
And in between the canaries injected, you'll see there is a number.

78
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:43,700
This number is used to pair the prototype pollution source with the gadget to produce a more accurate exploit.

79
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:51,520
The string prototype pollution follows a number and identifies that the value came from a prototype pollution source.

80
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:56,520
After that, the actual gadget name in this case is called HTML.

81
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:02,540
The second way that you can scan for gadgets is to change the settings.

82
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:11,020
This is useful when you might not have a prototype pollution source, but you want to ensure your site doesn't have any gadgets.

83
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:17,700
Let's head over to the DomInvader settings in the top right of the screen and let's switch it on.

84
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:30,600
So here we need to click the prototype pollution settings cog here, which will show you additional options.

85
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:35,760
And we need to choose scan for gadgets.

86
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:38,480
So let's click the scan for gadgets checkbox.

87
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:46,520
And what DomInvader does is it shows you the optimal settings for that selection.

88
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:59,440
So for example, it will try to remove the CSP and X-Frame options headers by default because iFrames are used to scan for gadgets.

89
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:02,760
You can change the defaults if you wish.

90
00:09:04,340 --> 00:09:10,980
But now when you refresh the page by clicking on the reload button, after you've clicked save,

91
00:09:12,040 --> 00:09:14,760
DomInvader will then scan for just gadgets.

92
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:34,000
Now we know how to find gadgets, let's try and exploit them.

93
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:38,440
First we need to follow the steps in the finding gadgets video to find our gadget.

94
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:43,600
Once you have your gadget, we simply need to click the green exploit button.

95
00:09:44,180 --> 00:09:45,860
So let's click the exploit button.

96
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:53,220
The exploit button will open a new window and attempt to match up the prototype pollution source with the gadget and sync

97
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:55,620
and create a prototype pollution payload.

98
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:58,520
If successful, you should see an alert.

99
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:03,520
We've successfully exploited prototype pollution with just a couple of clicks.

100
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:14,780
Some sites might not work correctly if you inject certain parts of the URL.

101
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:20,200
Perhaps they have a redirect when the hash is modified, which can break an injection.

102
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:26,640
For this reason, DomInvader allows you to customise where prototype pollution gets injected.

103
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:30,840
So let's head over to the DomInvader settings by clicking on the icon.

104
00:10:32,520 --> 00:10:36,160
And then we need to click on the prototype pollution settings cog here.

105
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:43,280
You can see there are three different checkboxes to control prototype pollution injection.

106
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:48,600
We have query string injection, hash injection and JSON injection.

107
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:56,980
Turning off the query string injection will prevent DomInvader for injecting in the query string.

108
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:01,120
Hash injection will do the same but in the hash portion of the URL.

109
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:09,900
And finally, switching off JSON injection will prevent DomInvader for injecting the prototype pollution inside of web messages.

110
00:11:09,900 --> 00:11:14,500
To confirm these settings, you just need to click on the save button.

111
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:20,240
And now DomInvader will only inject in the places that you've selected.

112
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:33,980
You might encounter a site where prototype pollution fails if all prototype pollution techniques are attempted.

113
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:38,600
DomInvader allows you to choose which techniques are used.

114
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:51,320
If you go to the settings icon and click the prototype pollution settings cog, you'll notice there's a techniques button.

115
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:59,180
Clicking on this button will open a dialog which allows you to switch off prototype pollution techniques.

116
00:12:02,700 --> 00:12:07,840
Once you are done, click save to save your changes and hit reload.

117
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:13,180
Now DomInvader will only try a single technique.

118
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:25,440
DomInvader, by default, will scan for prototype pollution in the top frame.

119
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:31,680
We found whilst testing that this offered the best performance and worked on most sites.

120
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:42,300
However, you might encounter situations where a site might block a certain technique but might be vulnerable to a different one.

121
00:12:42,860 --> 00:12:49,300
In this case, DomInvader can scan every technique selected in a separate frame.

122
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:55,040
To do this, click on the DomInvader icon and the prototype pollution cog.

123
00:12:58,540 --> 00:13:03,200
Then check the scan each technique in separate frame option.

124
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,200
And hit save.

125
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:19,380
Now, when DomInvader scans for prototype pollution, it will use iframes for each of the techniques, allowing you to scan them independently.

126
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:21,660
Let's try running the scan.

127
00:13:21,660 --> 00:13:29,460
You'll notice now a progress bar appears showing DomInvader scanning for sources.

128
00:13:29,460 --> 00:13:35,300
On completion, it will inform you how many prototype pollution sources it has found.

129
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:47,220
By default, DomInvader will scale the amount of properties scanned automatically.

130
00:13:47,780 --> 00:13:54,040
This produces faster performance but can miss gadgets if an injected property causes an exception.

131
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:00,240
For this reason, we allow you to customise how many properties per frame when scanning.

132
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:06,840
So, head over to the DomInvader settings and the prototype pollution settings cog.

133
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:12,120
Auto scale amount of properties per frame.

134
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:14,720
So, we need to switch that off.

135
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:20,340
And this allows you to customise how many properties per frame we want to scan.

136
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:24,160
So, if you drag it right, that's faster but less accurate.

137
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:27,260
And if you drag it left, it's slower but more accurate.

138
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:30,660
And we need to select scan for gadgets.

139
00:14:32,220 --> 00:14:40,700
And if you click save, that will enable you to change the amount of properties per frame DomInvader uses to scan.

140
00:14:46,580 --> 00:14:49,920
Whilst testing DomInvader on real world size,

141
00:14:50,340 --> 00:14:55,560
we noticed that we weren't really using the sources like location much.

142
00:14:55,560 --> 00:14:58,180
And we found when scanning for gadgets,

143
00:14:58,460 --> 00:15:03,700
we were getting a lot of noise from non-interesting syncs such as JSON.parse.

144
00:15:04,160 --> 00:15:11,820
We decided it would be a good idea to allow the user to customise which sources or syncs are instrumented.

145
00:15:12,380 --> 00:15:17,800
We decided to switch off all sources and non-interesting syncs by default.

146
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:23,640
If you want to customise which sources or syncs DomInvader instruments,

147
00:15:24,040 --> 00:15:26,100
